questions.question_text:
The slope field of $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2x-y$ is being drawn on the grid below.
What is the gradient of the line element at the marked point $(1,-2)$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\fill (1,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below right,font=\small] at (1.05,-2.05) {$(1,-2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.solution_text:
$\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2x-y$
$=2(1)-(-2)$
$=2+2$
$=4$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-3.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\draw[thick] (0.915,-2.34) -- (1.085,-1.66);
\fill (1,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[right,font=\small] at (1.2,-2) {gradient $4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.question_text:
The slope field below has identical line elements all the way along each horizontal line.
Which of the following equations could describe this field?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-2.9,0) -- (2.9,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-2.9) -- (0,2.9) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-2,-1,1,2} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.55) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-2,-1,1,2} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.55,\t) {$\t$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} \draw ({\x-0.111},-1.668) -- ({\x+0.111},-2.332);
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} \draw ({\x-0.157},-0.687) -- ({\x+0.157},-1.313);
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} \draw ({\x-0.247},0.247) -- ({\x+0.247},-0.247);
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} \draw ({\x-0.35},1) -- ({\x+0.35},1);
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} \draw ({\x-0.247},1.753) -- ({\x+0.247},2.247);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.solution_text:
Line elements are horizontal where the gradient is $0$.
$\dfrac{dy}{dx}=0$
$x+2y-4=0$
$2y=4-x$
$y=-\dfrac{1}{2}x+2$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-2,-1) grid (4,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-2.4,0) -- (4.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-2,-1,1,2,3,4} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\draw[thick,dashed,domain=-2:4,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-\x/2}) node[pos=0.8,above right,font=\small] {$y=-\frac{1}{2}x+2$};
\foreach \x/\y in {-2/3,0/2,2/1,4/0} \draw[thick] ({\x-0.35},{\y}) -- ({\x+0.35},{\y});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.question_text:
The slope field of $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=1-x$ is shown below.
Which statement correctly describes the solution curve passing through $(1,2)$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-1,-1) grid (3,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw (-1.157,{\y-0.313}) -- (-0.843,{\y+0.313});
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw (-0.247,{\y-0.247}) -- (0.247,{\y+0.247});
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw (0.65,{\y}) -- (1.35,{\y});
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw (1.753,{\y+0.247}) -- (2.247,{\y-0.247});
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw (2.843,{\y+0.313}) -- (3.157,{\y-0.313});
\fill (1,2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above right,font=\small] at (1,2) {$(1,2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.solution_text:
At $(1,2)$, $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=1-1=0$, so the curve has a stationary point.
For $x<1$, $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=1-x>0$, so the curve is increasing.
For $x>1$, $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=1-x<0$, so the curve is decreasing.
A curve that increases before a stationary point and decreases after it has a local maximum at $(1,2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-1,-1) grid (3,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] (-1.157,{\y-0.313}) -- (-0.843,{\y+0.313});
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] (-0.247,{\y-0.247}) -- (0.247,{\y+0.247});
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] (0.65,{\y}) -- (1.35,{\y});
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] (1.753,{\y+0.247}) -- (2.247,{\y-0.247});
\foreach \y in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] (2.843,{\y+0.313}) -- (3.157,{\y-0.313});
\draw[thick,domain=-1.2:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-\x*\x/2+\x+1.5});
\fill (1,2) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above,font=\small] at (1,2.05) {$(1,2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.question_text:
The slope field of $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2-y$ has a constant solution $y=2$.
What happens to the solution curve passing through $(0,0)$ as $x$ increases without bound?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-1,-1) grid (3,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.111},-1.332) -- ({\x+0.111},-0.668);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.157},-0.313) -- ({\x+0.157},0.313);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.247},0.753) -- ({\x+0.247},1.247);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.35},2) -- ({\x+0.35},2);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.247},3.247) -- ({\x+0.247},2.753);
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.solution_text:
At $(0,0)$, $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2-0=2>0$, so the curve starts by rising.
For any point with $0\le y<2$, $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2-y>0$, so $y$ continues to increase.
Because two solution curves of a first order differential equation can never cross, the curve cannot cross $y=2$.
As $y$ approaches $2$, the gradient $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=2-y$ approaches $0$, so the curve flattens out and approaches $y=2$ as a horizontal asymptote.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-1,-1) grid (3,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.111},-1.332) -- ({\x+0.111},-0.668);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.157},-0.313) -- ({\x+0.157},0.313);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.247},0.753) -- ({\x+0.247},1.247);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.35},2) -- ({\x+0.35},2);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.247},3.247) -- ({\x+0.247},2.753);
\draw[thick,domain=-0.4:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-2*exp(-\x)});
\draw[dashed] (-1,2) -- (3.3,2) node[right,font=\small] {$y=2$};
\fill (0,0) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.question_text:
The slope field below has vertical line elements along the $x$-axis ($y=0$) and horizontal line elements along the $y$-axis ($x=0$).
In the first quadrant ($x>0, y>0$), all line elements slope upwards.
Which differential equation corresponds to this field?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-2,-2) grid (2,2);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-2.9,0) -- (2.9,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-2.9) -- (0,2.9) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-2,-1,1,2} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.55) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-2,-1,1,2} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.55,\t) {$\t$};
\foreach \y in {-2,-1,1,2} \draw (-0.35,{\y}) -- (0.35,{\y});
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,1,2} \draw ({\x},-0.35) -- ({\x},0.35);
\foreach \x/\y in {1/2,-1/-2} \draw ({\x-0.340},{\y-0.085}) -- ({\x+0.340},{\y+0.085});
\foreach \x/\y in {-1/2,1/-2} \draw ({\x-0.340},{\y+0.085}) -- ({\x+0.340},{\y-0.085});
\foreach \x/\y in {1/1,2/2,-1/-1,-2/-2} \draw ({\x-0.313},{\y-0.157}) -- ({\x+0.313},{\y+0.157});
\foreach \x/\y in {-1/1,-2/2,1/-1,2/-2} \draw ({\x-0.313},{\y+0.157}) -- ({\x+0.313},{\y-0.157});
\foreach \x/\y in {2/1,-2/-1} \draw ({\x-0.247},{\y-0.247}) -- ({\x+0.247},{\y+0.247});
\foreach \x/\y in {-2/1,2/-1} \draw ({\x-0.247},{\y+0.247}) -- ({\x+0.247},{\y-0.247});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Consider the differential equation $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y(y-2)$.
The slope field has two constant solutions, $y=0$ and $y=2$.
A solution curve passes through the point $(0,1)$.
What is the limiting value of $y$ as $x\to\infty$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-1,-1) grid (3,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.111},-1.332) -- ({\x+0.111},-0.668);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.35},0) -- ({\x+0.35},0);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.247},1.247) -- ({\x+0.247},0.753);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.35},2) -- ({\x+0.35},2);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw ({\x-0.111},2.668) -- ({\x+0.111},3.332);
\fill (0,1) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

questions.solution_text:
For $0<y<2$, $y>0$ and $y-2<0$, so $\dfrac{dy}{dx}=y(y-2)<0$.
Because the gradient is negative throughout the band $0<y<2$, the solution curve through $(0,1)$ strictly decreases as $x$ increases.
Because $y=0$ is a constant solution and solution curves cannot cross, $y$ remains strictly greater than $0$ for all $x$.
Therefore, as $x\to\infty$, $y$ decreases asymptotically towards $0$.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[gray!30,very thin] (-1,-1) grid (3,3);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-1.4,0) -- (3.4,0) node[below] {$x$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,-1.4) -- (0,3.4) node[left] {$y$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (\t,-0.4) {$\t$};
\foreach \t in {-1,1,2,3} \node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-0.4,\t) {$\t$};
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.111},-1.332) -- ({\x+0.111},-0.668);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.35},0) -- ({\x+0.35},0);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.247},1.247) -- ({\x+0.247},0.753);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.35},2) -- ({\x+0.35},2);
\foreach \x in {-1,0,1,2,3} \draw[gray!70] ({\x-0.111},2.668) -- ({\x+0.111},3.332);
\draw[thick,domain=-0.6:3.2,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2/(1+exp(2*\x))});
\draw[dashed] (-1,2) -- (3.3,2) node[right,font=\small] {$y=2$};
\draw[dashed] (-1,0) -- (3.3,0);
\node[font=\small,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (2.4,0.55) {$y=0$};
\fill (0,1) circle (2.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
